###############################################################################

#
# Windows overrides for the snug options file. Read by `snug` on a Windows
# host *in addition to* `snug.options`, and it wins on any flag both files
# set. Precedence, highest first: command line, this file, `snug.options`.
#
# So this file is a PARTIAL override, not a second copy. Only the values that
# genuinely differ on Windows belong here; everything else - name, company,
# version, min-java, main-class, icon, splash, jvm-arg, localisations - still
# comes from `snug.options`. Keeping it to the differing lines is the whole
# point: a full second copy is how two files drift apart.
#
# The macOS counterpart is `snug.macos.options`, and Linux's is
# `snug.linux.options`. Only the file matching the host is ever read, so the
# others can sit side by side in version control without affecting each other.
# A missing one is not an error and is not reported.
#
# `--options <path>` bypasses this file entirely: naming a file means that
# file only, so it is also the way to guarantee a build's options do not
# depend on which machine ran it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one value that differs: the output artefact
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The output extension is what selects the artefact snug builds. A `.exe`
# is a Windows PE, which is what a Windows host must produce. A macOS host
# needs a `.app` *bundle* instead - a directory holding a Mach-O launcher
# plus its payload - so the same value cannot serve both, which is why it
# lives here rather than in the shared snug.options.
#
# scripts\build-release.cmd runs
#
#     target\release\snug.exe assets\snug-javafx-demo.jar
#
# with no -o and no --options, relying on this file being found from the
# working directory, and then asserts that the EXE landed at exactly the
# path below. It does not read the value itself, so the two cannot drift:
# this file is the only place the string exists.
#
# If this file is ever missing, snug falls back to its own default of
# <jar-stem>.exe in the working directory - so the file name would be right
# but the directory would not, and build-release.cmd fails on the missing
# assets\... path rather than quietly producing a stray EXE in the repo root.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

--input assets/snug-javafx-demo.jar
--output assets/snug-javafx-demo.exe
